  RELEVANCE: The most important recent English-language scholarly collection
  directly addressing the Addresses, bringing together both philosophical
  and political perspectives on the text central to the reception question.
  Contains essays by Breazeale, Farr, Schaefer, and others that represent
  the current state of the field on Fichte's nationalism and philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Pluralistic volume representing apologetic, critical, and
  contextualist readings in dialogue.
  },
  keywords = {Addresses, nationalism, education, reception, collection, Medium}
}

@book{nakhimovsky2011closed,
  author = {Nakhimovsky, Isaac},
  title = {The Closed Commercial State: Perpetual Peace and Commercial Society from Rousseau to Fichte},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  year = {2011},
  doi = {10.23943/princeton/9780691148946.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nakhimovsky reconstructs the intellectual context of
  Fichte's Der geschlossne Handelsstaat (1800), arguing that it represents
  a serious response to the problem of perpetual peace and commercial society
  developed from Rousseau through Kant. Fichte's vision of a closed national
  economy is shown to be a principled attempt to achieve international peace
  by removing the commercial competition that causes war, rather than a
  straightforwardly autarchic or proto-totalitarian economic program.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses one of the principal critical objections
  to Fichte's political thought --- the authoritarianism of his Closed
  Commercial State --- by placing it in the context of post-Kantian peace
  theory. Crucial for assessing the ethics and politics of Fichte's
  historical thought about state, economy, and international order.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative-contextualist: defends the intellectual coherence
  of Fichte's political economy against the anti-liberal charge.
  },
  keywords = {closed-commercial-state, political-economy, perpetual-peace, Rousseau, rehabilitation, Medium}
}

@article{fuchs2010founder,
  author = {Fuchs, Erich},
  title = {Fichte the Founder of German Nationalism? Contribution to the Understanding of the Reden an die deutsche Nation},
  year = {2010},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fuchs argues that the image of Fichte as the founder of
  German nationalism is a historically constructed prejudice. The Reden
  were written by Fichte as an Enlightenment and cosmopolitan thinker for
  purposes of spiritual and political rebirth after the defeat of 1806,
  then put aside during the Restoration; only during the Bismarckian Reich
  were they reinterpreted on a nationalist key and transformed into the
  founding text of pan-German nationalism. The educational project of the
  Reden, Fuchs contends, can only be understood within the context of
  Fichte's overall transcendental system.

  RELEVANCE: Classic reception-history argument for the systematic distortion
  of Fichte's historical-political thought by nationalist appropriation.
  Identifies the specific historical moment (Bismarckian Reich) at which
  the ideological transformation occurred.

  POSITION: Apologetic-historical: defends Fichte's original intentions
  against the nationalist myth by periodizing the reception.

  INCOMPLETE
  },
  keywords = {reception-history, nationalism, Bismarck, Enlightenment, Reden, Low}
}
